intent: Opens the visible phase list in T3's terms (update_plan in Codex, the reply in Claude Code) and keeps it current. Preserve the six phase names and that it opens before launching anything.
--- upstream
Open a todolist with one entry per phase (Frame, Fan out, Cross-judge, Pick, Graft, Verify) before launching anything.
--- patched
Open a step list with one entry per phase (Frame, Fan out, Cross-judge, Pick, Graft, Verify) before launching anything, and keep it current at each phase. Codex keeps it with `update_plan`. Claude Code keeps it in the reply.

=== entry d4a7c6b535e0
kind: translation
rules: model-role, model-effort
intent: Runners come from the `arena runners` line in the project models file, else willyskills's default, applied with the model-entry snippet; the rejected-slug and family-prefix fallbacks, the dropped-seat top-up that only follows a rejected slug, and the speed/reasoning sentence are dropped. Families are no longer defined here; Phase C defines a family as the entry's provider. Preserve the frame steps, the more-runners and same-model-N-times guidance, and the per-candidate output location.
--- upstream
1. State the artifact each candidate is producing.
2. Derive the rubric, 3-6 concrete gradeable criteria for what success looks like on *this* task. It is the picker's tool in Phase D. Candidates only see the task.
3. Pick the runners. Use the `arena runners` line in `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`. If the rule or that line is missing, default to one each on `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` and `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`. An `auto` or `inherit-parent` entry in this line means the parent model, so omit `model` for it. If the Task tool rejects a configured entry, run that seat on its family's default and say so. Families go by prefix: `claude-*`, and `grok-*` with `cursor-grok-*` and `composer-*`. A refused `composer-*` entry runs on `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`. If the family has no default, or the Task tool rejects that default, use the closest valid slug of the same family from its error message, or drop the seat and say so when there is none. If dropped seats leave fewer than two runners, add runners until there are two, or one when the line has one entry. Run each added runner on a remaining runner's model, or omit `model` so it runs on the parent model when none remain, and say so. Spawn more runners when the arena covers multiple design directions. Use the same model N times when the work is generation-bound rather than judgment-sensitive. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section of the **poteto-mode** skill.
4. Give each candidate its own output location (a git worktree where possible, otherwise `/tmp/arena-<slug>/candidate-<n>/`), per the **separate-before-serializing-shared-state** principle skill.
--- patched
1. State the artifact each candidate is producing.
2. Derive the rubric, 3-6 concrete gradeable criteria for what success looks like on *this* task. It is the picker's tool in Phase D. Candidates only see the task.
3. Pick the runners. Use the `arena runners` line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else {{model:arena runners}}, one runner per entry. {{snippet:model-entry}} Spawn more runners when the arena covers multiple design directions. Use the same model N times when the work is generation-bound rather than judgment-sensitive.
4. Give each candidate its own output location (a git worktree where possible, otherwise `/tmp/arena-<slug>/candidate-<n>/`), per the **separate-before-serializing-shared-state** principle skill.

=== entry ea996dc046fd
kind: translation
rules: delegate, background, isolation
intent: Fans out N candidates as N async delegate_task calls in one message, each writer in its own worktree, and ends the turn until completions wake the parent. Preserve what each brief carries: task, shared grounding path, own output path, artifact plus rationale with rejected alternatives.
--- upstream
Spawn all N subagents in one message with `run_in_background: true`, each with the task, the path to the shared grounding, its own output path, and instructions to produce the artifact and a short rationale naming the alternatives it considered and what it rejected.
--- patched
Spawn all N subagents in one message: N `delegate_task` calls with `mode: "async"`, each with its own `clientRequestId` and, when its output location is a worktree, `isolation: "worktree"`. A worktree starts from the parent's current local commit without uncommitted edits, so commit the shared grounding first or give it by absolute path. Each `task` holds the whole brief: the task, the path to the shared grounding, its own output path, and instructions to produce the artifact and a short rationale naming the alternatives it considered and what it rejected. Keep each `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Each completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling.

=== entry 58640ec1e893
kind: translation
rules: model-role, delegate, read-only, background
intent: The cross-judge comes from the `arena cross-judge pool` line in the project models file, else willyskills's default, and is a read-only async delegate_task child. Upstream now requires a judge from another family (tried in pool order, aliases never judge, the default pool's other-family entries next, else skip the judge and say so, and a note when the parent's family is unknown); all of that is kept, with family defined as the entry's provider since the slug-prefix families are dropped. Dropped: the rejected-slug fallback (closest level or speed) and the default-pool examples that name Claude and Grok slugs. Rule gap: with willyskills's default pool (one claudeAgent entry) a claudeAgent parent gets no judge unless the project line adds another provider. Preserve what the judge sees and returns, that it runs alongside Phase D, and that it waits for all candidates.
--- upstream
After all Phase B candidates complete, choose one model from the `arena cross-judge pool` line in `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`. If the rule or that line is missing, choose from `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` and `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`. Spawn one readonly judge subagent. The judge is never from the parent's family, as Phase A step 3 defines families. Try only the entries from another family, in pool order. An `auto` or `inherit-parent` entry is the parent model, so it never judges. With the default pool, a Claude parent gets `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`. A parent from any other family gets `claude-opus-5-5-xhigh` first. If the Task tool rejects an entry, use a valid slug of the same model at the closest level or speed from the error message, or move to the next entry when there is none. If no entry from another family runs, try the default pool's entries from another family the same way. If none of those runs, skip the judge, pick the base alone in Phase D, and say so. If you do not know the parent's model, try the pool's real slugs in pool order and say that the family check did not run. The judge sees the rubric and the candidates by path label, scores each criterion, and recommends a base with rationale. It runs in parallel with your reading in Phase D. Don't spawn it while candidates are still writing.
--- patched
After all Phase B candidates complete, choose one entry from the `arena cross-judge pool` line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else from {{model:arena cross-judge pool}}. Spawn one judge subagent on that entry with `delegate_task`, `mode: "async"`, and no `isolation`. Its brief says "Do not edit, create, commit, or push files.", and you run `git status` afterward. The judge is never from the parent's family, and an entry's family is its provider. Try only the entries from another family, in pool order. An `inherit-parent` entry is the parent model, so it never judges. If the line has no entry from another family, try the entries of {{model:arena cross-judge pool}} from another family the same way. If none of those runs, skip the judge, pick the base alone in Phase D, and say so. If you do not know the parent's provider, try the pool's entries other than `inherit-parent` in pool order and say that the family check did not run. The judge sees the rubric and the candidates by path label, scores each criterion, and recommends a base with rationale. It runs in parallel with your reading in Phase D. Don't spawn it while candidates are still writing.

=== entry 8b5a47bda9b3
kind: translation
rules: invoke
intent: The description names both invocations, /arena for Claude Code and $arena for Codex. Everything else stays verbatim.
--- upstream
---
name: arena
description: "Spawn N parallel candidates at the same task, pick a base, graft the strongest parts of the losers into it. Use for /arena, 'arena this', 'throw it in the arena', or when one attempt at a non-trivial artifact would lock in the wrong shape."
disable-model-invocation: true
---
--- patched
---
name: arena
description: "Spawn N parallel candidates at the same task, pick a base, graft the strongest parts of the losers into it. Use for /arena or $arena, 'arena this', 'throw it in the arena', or when one attempt at a non-trivial artifact would lock in the wrong shape."
disable-model-invocation: true
